statement:
Find the smallest positive integer $k$ for which there exists a colouring of the positive integers $\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ with $k$ colours and a function $f : \mathbb{Z}_{>0} \to \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ with the following two properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] For all positive integers $m$, $n$ of the same colour, $f(m + n) = f(m) + f(n)$.
\item[(ii)] There are positive integers $m$, $n$ such that $f(m + n) \neq f(m) + f(n)$.
\end{enumerate}
(In a colouring of $\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ with $k$ colours, every integer is coloured in exactly one of the $k$ colours. In both (i) and (ii) the positive integers $m$, $n$ are not necessarily distinct.)

Let $n \geq 1$ be an integer and let $t_1 < t_2 < \cdots < t_n$ be positive integers. In a group of $t_n + 1$ people, some games of chess are played. Two people can play each other at most once. Prove that it is possible for the following two conditions to hold at the same time:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] The number of games played by each person is one of $t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_n$.
\item[(ii)] For every $i$ with $1 \leq i \leq n$, there is someone who has played exactly $t_i$ games of chess.
\end{enumerate}
